purpose: HTTP, cookies, and the socket layer. Everything that touches the wire.

# net sits above url and privacy: guidelines/privacy.md requires every request to
# have passed privacy first, which net enforces by taking a privacy::Verdict.
allow: util url privacy

public: Hpack.h Http2Frames.h Http2Session.h HttpMessage.h CookieJar.h Transport.h HttpCache.h Fetch.h RequestQueue.h SocketTransport.h ContentEncoding.h ConnectionPool.h Cors.h WebSocketFrames.h WebSocketConnection.h EventStream.h EventSourceConnection.h ResolverCache.h

# ADR 0001 sanctions OpenSSL for the TLS record layer only -- we own HTTP
# entirely. Exactly one file names it, SocketTransport.cpp, and this line is
# what keeps that true rather than aspirational.
extern: openssl

max_tu_lines: 800

budget: WebSocketConnection header_lines=145 public_methods=13 members=19
budget: EventSourceConnection header_lines=115 public_methods=14 members=13
budget: HttpHeaders    header_lines=40 public_methods=16 members=1
# HPACK's decoder *is* the connection's compression state, which is why it is a
# class at all and why it has four members: the dynamic table, its accounted
# size, the capacity the peer last asked for, and the reason it failed. None of
# the four is derivable from the others and all four are attacker-influenced,
# which is the entire content of RFC 7541's CVE history. Growing this list means
# adding state a peer can steer, and that is worth a commit message.
budget: Decoder        header_lines=50 public_methods=8 members=4
# One HTTP/2 connection. Nineteen members, and the number is the protocol
# rather than the implementation: a connection that carries many requests at
# once has to hold, at minimum, the socket, the HPACK decoder that is the
# connection's and not any stream's, the peer's settings, both halves of the
# outgoing buffer, the incoming buffer, the streams, the four fields of a header
# block being assembled across CONTINUATIONs, two flow-control windows, the next
# stream id, the last one a GOAWAY promised, and whether it has failed.
#
# The one to watch is the header-block group. `assembling_`, `header_block_`,
# `assembling_end_stream_` and `continuations_` exist because HPACK cannot be
# paused mid-field, so a block split across frames has to be reassembled before
# it is decoded -- and while it is being reassembled no other frame may arrive.
# Anything added there is state a peer steers.
#
# Implemented in two translation units, split by whose state each function
# touches: Http2Session.cpp owns the socket and the connection, Http2Streams.cpp
# owns one request and one response. That is the split `Engine` and `Page`
# already carry, and here it also separates the failures that cost one request
# from the failures that cost every request on the socket.
# Raised to 195 by the comment on `Request`, which is nine lines saying why its
# four fields are owned strings rather than views. That comment is the record of
# a bug that made every server on the web refuse this browser while the suite
# stayed green, and it is cheaper here than rediscovered.
budget: Http2Session   header_lines=195 public_methods=17 members=19
# Raised from 60 header lines by ADR 0010's connection reuse. The three new
# questions -- was the body self-delimited, is anything left after it, has
# anything arrived at all -- are all things only the parser knows, and all
# three are what decides whether a connection can carry a second request. The
# alternative was for Fetch to keep its own copy of the framing, which is two
# answers to one question.
budget: ResponseParser header_lines=70 public_methods=16 members=10
budget: Cookie         header_lines=50 public_methods=10 members=10
budget: CookieJar      header_lines=60 public_methods=18 members=2
budget: HttpCache      header_lines=45 public_methods=14 members=3
# Raised to 50 by the partition key on `StartConnect`. The five lines are the
# comment saying why a name lookup is keyed by partition and not by host, which
# is the only thing standing between a resolver cache and a cross-site oracle.
# Raised to 70 lines by `NegotiatedProtocol`. Its comment is most of the
# increase and is load-bearing: the answer comes from the *server*, during the
# handshake, so a socket has to be opened before anyone can find out whether it
# should have been shared. That timing is the whole difficulty of putting
# HTTP/2 behind a connection pool.
budget: Transport      header_lines=70 public_methods=13 members=0
# Names already resolved, keyed by the ADR 0005 partition key. One member: the
# entry list. The TTL and the bound are constants beside it rather than state,
# because neither is a decision this class gets to make per instance.
budget: ResolverCache  header_lines=30 public_methods=6 members=1
# The real transport's factory. Two members: the options it was built with and
# the resolver cache every transport it makes shares. The cache is owned here
# rather than being a process-wide static so that it has a lifetime and a test
# can ask for a cold one.
budget: SocketTransportFactory header_lines=35 public_methods=6 members=2
# One request, in flight. Sixteen members is a lot and every one of them is a
# line of what used to be a stack frame: the verdict, the four collaborators,
# the options that a redirect rewrites, the connection, the parser, the
# half-sent buffer, and the four counters that used to be locals in a while
# loop. That is what turning a call into an object costs, and the alternative
# -- keeping the loop and blocking in it -- is what ADR 0011 rejected.
#
# Raised from 17 to 19 members and from 85 to 100 header lines by ADR 0010's
# connection reuse. The two new members are `reused_` and `retried_`, and they
# are one fact each: whether the connection in hand came out of the pool, and
# whether the single retry that fact buys has been spent. Neither can be
# derived -- the pool has already forgotten which connection it handed over,
# and by the time a send fails there is nothing left to ask.
#
# Raised to 24 members and 150 lines by HTTP/2 (ADR 0032). Five, and they are
# one fact each: the shared session and this request's stream on it, the pool
# key and whether this request holds that origin's connect claim, and the
# header *list* -- kept rather than the serialized request, because both
# protocols send the same fields and which framing to use is not known until
# ALPN has answered. That last one is what keeps `Fetch` from having two copies
# of what a request contains.
budget: FetchRequest   header_lines=150 public_methods=17 members=24
# Connections kept between requests, keyed by the ADR 0005 partition key. Two
# members: the factory it falls back to, and the idle list. Everything else in
# it is policy about when to let one go.
#
# Raised to 5 members by HTTP/2 (ADR 0032), and every one of the three is the
# protocol rather than the implementation. `sessions_` is the second thing this
# pool now hands out and the first it hands out *shared*. `claims_` is the
# coalescing: at most one connect per origin while nobody knows which protocol
# it speaks, because ALPN answers after the socket is open and six requests
# would otherwise open six. `protocols_` is what takes that bound off again the
# moment the answer is known -- bounded and partition-keyed, because "has this
# browser learned that example.com speaks HTTP/2" is a question about where the
# user has been.
budget: ConnectionPool header_lines=145 public_methods=18 members=5
# The scheduler in front of Fetch. Four collaborators, three lists (started,
# waiting on a slot, finished) and a counter. The three lists are the shape of
# the problem: a bound per partition means a request can exist without a
# connection, and a completion can exist after the request that made it is gone.
#
# Raised to 155 lines, 17 methods and 9 members by ADR 0020 §2. The ninth
# member is the preflight cache and it is here rather than beside the HTTP
# cache for the reason the connection pool is here: it is keyed by the ADR 0005
# partition key, and this is the only class that holds both a request and the
# key it is being made under. The method is `Cancel`, which `AbortController`
# needs and which `CancelAll` -- the right hammer for a page going away -- is
# no help at all for. The lines are the two structs a preflight made necessary:
# a request that has to ask permission first is *two* exchanges under one id,
# so what used to be three parallel fields on `Queued` is now a `Pending` value
# that can be held while the `OPTIONS` runs.
# Raised to 160 lines by the URL an active request carries for the load
# timeline, which is filled in only when the timeline is on.
budget: RequestQueue   header_lines=160 public_methods=17 members=9
# What a preflight bought, remembered so the next request does not buy it
# again. One member -- the entries -- and the bound on how many there may be is
# a constant beside it rather than a policy knob: every key in this table comes
# from a page.
budget: PreflightCache header_lines=35 public_methods=6 members=1
